@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Documentation Guidelines";
}
@section css
{
    @Styles.Render("~/bundles/controls/css/aspmvc")
}
<div class="mdi-action-info help" title="More information">
    <ul class="info">
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span> Guidelines that you should never skip and should be applicable to all situations.</li>
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">**</span> Strongly recommended guidelines.</li>
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">*</span> Recommended guidelines that may not be applicable in all situations.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
<div class="col-sm-12" id="doc-content">
    <ul class="rules unordered">
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title first"><span class="rule-item">OW9000 -</span> Write comments and documentation in US English <span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW9001 -</span> Avoid inline comments <span class="badge lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>
                If you feel the need to explain a block of code using a comment, consider replacing that block with a method having a clear name.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW9002 -</span> Only write comments to explain complex algorithms or decisions <span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>
                Try to focus comments on the <i>why</i> and <i>what</i> of a code block and not the <i>how</i>. Avoid explaining the statements in words, 
                but instead help the reader understand why you chose a certain solution or algorithm and what you are trying to achieve. 
                If applicable, also mention that you chose an alternative solution because you ran into a problem with the obvious solution.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW9003 -</span> Don’t use comments for tracking work to be done later <span class="badge lbl-importance">*</span></h2>
            <p>
                Annotating a block of code or some work to be done using a TODO or similar comment may seem a reasonable way of tracking work-to-be-done.
                But in reality, nobody really searches for comments like that. Use a work item tracking system such as Team Foundation Server to keep track of left overs.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>
